..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1480:             cached in the ITB, the IDU uses the page frame number (PFN) and protection
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1481:             bits for the page that contains the VPC to complete the address translation
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1482:             and access checks.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1483: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1484:             The ITBs support a single address space number (ASN) through the PTE ASM
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1485:             bit. Each PTE entry in the ITB contains an ASM bit. Write operations to
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1486:             the ITBASM register invalidate all entries that do not have the ASM bit set.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1487:             This is a simple way to preserve entries that map operating system regions
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1488:             while invalidating all other entries. (The ITBASM register is described in
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1489:             Section 4.1.5.)
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1490: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1491:             For more information about TBs and PTEs, see the Alpha Architecture
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1492:             Reference Manual.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1493: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1494: 2.2.3 Interrupt Logic
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1495: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1496:             The IDU exception logic supports three sources of interrupts:
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1497: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1498:             ? Hardware interrupts
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1499: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1500:                   ? Three level-sensitive hardware interrupts are sourced by pins irq<2:0>.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1501: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1502:                   ? Two internally generated interrupts respond to external interface error
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1503:                        conditions. These are sourced by the memory controller error status
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1504:                        register (ESR, Section 5.6.6) and the I/O controller status register 0
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1505:                        (IOC_STAT0, Section 6.4.3).
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1506: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1507:             ? Software interrupts
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1508:                   The 15 prioritized software interrupts are sourced by the software interrupt
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1509:                   request register (SIRR, Section 4.1.13.2).
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1510: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1511:             ? Asynchronous system traps (ASTs)
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1512:                   Four ASTs are available, one for each processor mode: user, supervisor,
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1513:                   executive, and kernel. These traps are sourced by the asynchronous trap
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1514:                   request register (ASTRR, Section 4.1.13.3).
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1515: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1516:             In addition to the three level-sensitive hardware interrupts from the irq<2:0>
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1517:             pins, error conditions detected in the memory controller and the IOC can
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1518:             interrupt the CPU.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1519: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1520:                                                                                                        Internal Architecture 2?5
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1521:             The memory controller generates an interrupt if it detects any error condition,
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1522:             such as a nonexistent memory access, a missed refresh cycle, an uncorrectable
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1523:             ECC error, and a Bcache parity error. To return the memory subsystem to
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1524:             correct operation, software must clear these error conditions by writing to the
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1525:             memory controller ESR.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1526: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1527:             The I/O controller generates an interrupt to the CPU when it detects certain
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1528:             error conditions. These errors are logged in the IOC_STAT0 register. To
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1529:             remove the interrupt, software must write a one to the error bit in the
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1530:             IOC_STAT0 register.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1531: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1532:             The memory controller and IOC error conditions and registers are described in
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1533:             Chapters 5 and 6.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1534: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1535:             All interrupts can be independently masked using onchip enable registers.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1536:             In addition, AST interrupts are qualified by the current processor mode
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1537:             and the current state of software interrupt enable register bit 2 (SIER,
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1538:             Section 4.1.13.5). Providing distinct enable bits for each independent interrupt
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1539:             source allows a flexible, software-controlled interrupt priority scheme to be
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1540:             implemented in PALcode or the operating system.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1541: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1542:             For example, the microprocessor can support a 5-level interrupt priority
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1543:             scheme by defining a distinct state of the hardware interrupt enable register
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1544:             (HIER, Section 4.1.13.4) for each interrupt priority level (IPL). The state of the
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1545:             HIER determines the current interrupt priority. The lowest IPL is produced by
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1546:             enabling all five interrupts, for example bits <5:1>. The next IPL is produced
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1547:             by enabling bits <5:2> and so on, to the highest IPL that is produced by
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1548:             enabling only bit 5 and disabling bits <4:1>.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1549: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1550:             When all interrupt enable bits are cleared, the processor cannot be interrupted
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1551:             from the hardware interrupt request register (HIRR, Section 4.1.13.1). Each
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1552:             state (bits <5:1> enabled through only bit 5 enabled) represents an individual
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1553:             IPL. If these states are the only states allowed in the HIER, a 5-level hardware
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1554:             interrupt priority scheme can be controlled entirely by PAL software. The
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1555:             scheme is extensible to provide multiple interrupt sources at the same IPL by
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1556:             grouping enable bits. Groups of enable bits must be set and cleared together
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1557:             to support multiple interrupts of equal priority level. This method reduces the
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1558:             total available number of distinct levels.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1559: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1560:             Because enable bits are provided for all hardware, software, and AST interrupt
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1561:             requests, a priority scheme can span all sources of processor interrupts.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1562:             Additional restrictions are placed on AST interrupt requests.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1563: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1564: 2?6 Internal Architecture
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1565:             Four AST interrupts are provided; one for each processor mode. The AST
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1566:             interrupt requests are qualified such that AST requests that correspond to a
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1567:             given mode are blocked when the processor is in a higher mode, regardless of
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1568:             the state of the AST interrupt enable register (ASTER, Section 4.1.13.6). In
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1569:             addition, all AST interrupt requests are qualified by SIER bit 2.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1570: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1571:             When the processor receives an interrupt request and that request is enabled
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1572:             and the processor is not currently executing PALcode, the hardware reports or
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1573:             delivers an interrupt to the exception logic. Before vectoring to the interrupt-
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1574:             service PAL dispatch address, the pipeline is completely drained and all
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1575:             outstanding Dcache fills are completed. The restart address is saved in the
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1576:             exception address register (EXC_ADDR, Section 4.1.8) and the processor enters
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1577:             PALmode. The cause of the interrupt can be determined by examining the
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1578:             state of the interrupt request registers.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1579: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1580:             Usually, the interrupts from the irq<2:0> pins can be grouped as follows:
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1581: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1582:             ? Interval timer interrupts
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1583: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1584:             ? Halt, powerfail, ISA and EISA nonmaskable interrupts, and the PCI serr_l
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1585:                   signal
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1586: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1587:             ? ISA and EISA interrupt requests, and PCI interrupt requests
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1588: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1589:             Hardware interrupt requests are level-sensitive; therefore, they can be removed
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1590:             before an interrupt is serviced. If they are removed before the interrupt
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1591:             request register is read, the register will return a zero value.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1592: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1593: 2.2.4 Performance Counters
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1594: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:1595:             An onchip performance recording mechanism counts various hardware events
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:4459: 4.1.13 Interrupt Request and Enable Registers
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:4460: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:4461:             The interrupt request and enable registers comprise two sets of three
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:4462:             registers each. The read formats for the three interrupt request registers
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:4463:             are identical, as are the read formats for the three interrupt enable registers.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:4464:             Each read/write register has a unique write format.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:4465: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:4466:             Table 4?13 lists the interrupt request and enable register and the sections and
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:4467:             figures that describe them.
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:4468: 
..\_scratchpad\21066_66a_hrm.txt:4469:                                                            Internal Processor Registers 4?17
