4943: 4.1.13.6 Asynchronous Trap Interrupt Enable Register
4944:             The read and write asynchronous trap interrupt enable register (ASTER)
4945:             enables the AST interrupt requests described in Table 4-21. There is a one-to-
4946:             one correspondence between the interrupt request and enable bits. When set,
4947:             a bit in this register enables the corresponding interrupt request in the ASTRR
4948:             (Section 4.1.13.3); otherwise, the request is disabled.
4949: 
4950:             When read, the ASTER returns all the interrupt enables described in
4951:             Table 4-15.
4952: 
4953:             Figure 4-10 shows the ASTER read format, and Table 4-15 describes its fields.
4954:             Figure 4-15 shows the ASTER write format, and Table 4-21 describes its
4955:             fields.
4956: 
4957:                                       Internal Processor Registers 4-27
4958: Figure 4-15 ASTER Write Format
4959: 
4960: 63       52 51 50 49 48 47                       0
4961: 
4962:          USEK
4963: 
4964:     IGN  AAAA                      IGN
4965: 
4966:          EEEE
4967: 
4968: Table 4-21 ASTER Write-Format Field Description
4969: 
4970: Bits Field Type Description
4971: 
4972: 63:52 IGN RW                       Ignored when written.
4973: 
4974: 51       UAE RW                    User AST enable--When set, this bit enables user mode
4975:                                    AST interrupts.
4976: 50       SAE RW
4977:                                    Supervisor AST enable--When set, this bit enables
4978: 49       EAE RW                    supervisor mode AST interrupts.
4979: 
4980: 48       KAE RW                    Executive AST enable--When set, this bit enables executive
4981:                                    mode AST interrupts.
4982: 47:0 IGN RW
4983:                                    Kernel AST enable--When set, this bit enables kernel mode
4984:                                    AST interrupts.
4985: 
4986:                                    Ignored when written.
4987: 
4988: 4.1.14 Performance Counters
4989: 
4990:             The performance counters are reset to zero at power up; otherwise, they are
4991:             never cleared. The counters count events over a long period of time relative to
4992:             the event frequency; intermediate counter values cannot be extracted.
4993: 
4994:             Because the counters continuously accumulate a count of selected events,
4995:             despite interrupts being enabled, the first interrupt after selecting a new
4996:             counter input has an error boundary as large as the specified overflow range.
4997:             The overflow range is specified in bits 3 and 0 of the ICCSR (Section 4.1.7).
4998: 
4999:             Some inputs can overcount events that occur simultaneously with Dstream
5000:             errors that abort the event late in the pipeline. For example, when counting
5001:             load instructions, an attempt to execute a load instruction that results in
5002:             an ITB_MISS exception increments the performance counter after the first
5003:             aborted execution attempt, and then increments the counter again after the
5004:             TB-fill routine when the load instruction reissues and completes.
5005: 
5006: 4-28 Internal Processor Registers
5007: Performance counter interrupts are reported six cycles after the event
5008: that caused the counter to overflow. Additional delay can occur before an
5009: interrupt is serviced if the processor is executing PALcode that always disables
5010: interrupts. Events occurring during the interval between counter overflow and
5011: interrupt service are counted toward the next interrupt. An interrupt will be
5012: missed only in the case of complete counter wraparound while interrupts are
5013: disabled.
5014: 
5015: The six cycles before an interrupt is triggered implies that as many as 12
5016: instructions might have completed before the start of the interrupt service
5017: routine.
5018: 
5019: When counting Icache misses, intervening instructions cannot complete and the
5020: exception PC (EXC_ADDR <63:2>, Section 4.1.8) contains the address of the
5021: last Icache miss. Branch mispredictions allow no more than two instructions
5022: to complete before start of the interrupt service routine.
5023: 
5024: Table 4-22 lists performance counter 0 inputs and Table 4-23 lists performance
5025: counter 1 inputs.
